const stories = [{"id": "story-one", "title": "Runner Up!", "text": "Runner Up!\r\n\r\n\r\n2026: The StairMaster Incident\r\nJust 32.. more.. seconds. I huff and puff on the StairMaster, looking down at a pathetic time of 4 minutes and 28 seconds. I grip the handles harder, like that’ll magically make me stronger. Just make it to five, and you can get off, I tell myself. I’m hovering my finger over the stop button when I hear it.\r\n“Sareena? Oh my god. It has been too long!” A melodic voice calls out. \r\nFuck me. Please tell me that’s not who I think it is. I let my hand fall away from the button and keep it pushing. Can’t let her know my body craps out after 5 minutes of cardio. I plaster on a smile and turn.\r\n“Radhika! Wow, yeah. Long time.” I assess her while trying my best to hide my panting. She looks exactly the same. No—better. Like someone took the version of her from high school and refined it. Radhika 2.0 with sharpened edges and not one strand of hair out of place. Even her gym clothes look perfectly curated. Figures, she is a fitness content creator now. Something I really shouldn’t know but do due to my rabid habit of instastalking.\r\n“A little out of breath there, huh?” She smiles, but it doesn’t reach her eyes. I purse my lips and let out a chuckle just as fake. She effortlessly climbs on the machine and picks the highest speed.\r\n“So, how have things been? I wasn't expecting to see you back here.” I pause. Back here. As in, back home. Back to square one. I won’t be telling her the truth, which is that I’ve been in between jobs and had to move back to my parents place 6 months ago. At first, I tried to stay busy, but now even going to the gym feels like scaling a mountain.\r\n“Oh, you know, just working here and there.”\r\n“Oh really? Doing what?”\r\n“I babysit.” I fold, bracing for the psychological impact of the verbal dagger about to follow.\r\n“Aw. That’s… cute. I remember doing that in high school.” Of course you do. \r\n“I actually just got promoted at my job.” She says with a smug look. I widen my smile as insincerely as I can muster.\r\n“Congrats.”\r\n“By the way, what are you doing on Saturday?” Rotting, probably. \r\n“Probably gonna be back here!” I say with strained enthusiasm. Damn, 7 minutes in and I can feel my body giving up on me.\r\n“Are you sure about that? You’ve always hated cardio.” She says, smirking. Her fake, upbeat inflection never falters.\r\n“Well, always a pleasure to see you, Radhika.” I finally hit the stop button. Sweet relief at last.\r\n“Wait!” \r\n“Yes?” I freeze mid-dismount.\r\n“I’m entering a pageant. You should come for support. If you’re not too busy with… babysitting.”  I pause. Damn, being a literal workout influencer wasn’t enough for her? She’s entering a pageant now too? I look down at my own body, which has seen better days.\r\n“Yeah, maybe. I’ll let you know.”\r\n“Sure! And I’ll let Krish know you said hi next time I see him.” There it is. She cocks her head, clearly aware of the scathing implication behind it. Krish. My ex-boyfriend. Who she stole from me, of course. I have half a mind to tell her I never actually said hi. I don’t even reply to her as I speedwalk to the locker room. I had another 20 minutes of working out planned, but I just want to go home now.\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\n2016: Angel Numbers\r\n“2:22! Make a wish!” I whisper it like it’s a secret, even though there’s no one else in the room to hear us. Beside me, Radhika goes completely still. I turn my head. Her eyes are closed, her face serious in a way that makes me want to laugh.\r\n“Wait, you’re actually doing it?” I snort at her commitment to the bit.\r\nShe doesn’t reply, just presses her lips together in concentration. I close my eyes too, just in case. After a moment, I peek them open.\r\n“Okay, done.” She opens hers a second later. \r\n“What did you wish for?” I immediately ask, now intrigued.\r\nShe hesitates for a second, before breaking into her signature gap-toothed grin.\r\n“I wished for us to be best friends forever.” \r\n“That’s what I wished for too!” We erupt in giggles and scooch closer together.\r\n“That is so not what you wished for. You’re just copying me.”\r\n“It is too!” I huff. She can be so annoying sometimes. But honestly, she could push my buttons all day and I don’t think it would ever bother me. My mind starts to wander. Everything feels so easy with Radhu. It always has. Ever since that day we played mermaids on the playground, we’ve been attached at the hip. I don’t want this to ever change. I snap out of my thoughts when I feel her concerned gaze on me. \r\n“What’s wrong, Reena?” She must’ve noticed my expression growing serious. \r\n“Do you think we’ll still be friends when we grow up?” I blurt out. Stupid. She’s about to answer when a knock on the door from her mom interrupts us.\r\n“Girls. It’s two am. Go to sleep.” She lets the door close shut with an exasperated sigh.\r\n“Sorry Aunty.” I say at the same time Radhu says, “Sorry Amma.” \r\nWe glance at each other and can’t help but burst into quiet laughter, shoulders shaking. I sigh internally, glad that the awkward moment has passed. I burrow deeper into my sleeping bag, pulling it up to my chin. \r\n“I guess what I meant to say was,” I whisper, lowering my voice even more, like Aunty might somehow still hear us through the walls. “High school is supposed to change everything.”\r\n“It won’t,” she says, shaking her head. I tilt my head, studying her. She seems so sure. \r\n“You don’t even know what high school is like.” I can feel my anxiety seeping through my words. \r\n“I don’t need to,” she shrugs. “It’s not going to change this,” she says, wagging her finger with utmost certainty. \r\n“This,” I repeat, laughing softly. I don’t let it show, but part of me feels inexplicably relieved by that singular word. She nudges my arm through the sleeping bag. “You know what I mean.” I do. I just like making her say it.\r\n“What if we get different friends?” I press. “Or like… sit at different lunch tables?”\r\n“Then we’ll just have to combine our tables,” She says, confident as ever.\r\n“I don’t think it works like that,” I say, jabbing her shoulder teasingly. She just rolls her eyes. Suddenly, another doubt creeps into my mind.\r\n “Radhu… what if we get boyfriends?” Her face is half-hidden in the dark now, but I can still make out the thoughtful crease between her eyebrows. A second too long passes, and I open my mouth to say something, anything, to recover from the blunder. Before I can, she speaks. \r\n“Then we’ll still be friends,” She says quietly.\r\n“Okay, but what if your boyfriend doesn't like me?”  I counter.\r\n“Then I don’t like him,” She says, just as quickly. I blink at her.\r\n“That’s a little extreme,” I say, a small smile playing on my lips. She shrugs, like it’s obvious. \r\n“Why would I want to be with someone who doesn’t like you?” I feel something unexpectedly warm settling in my chest.\r\n“True,” I say, grinning. “You do have great taste.”\r\n“Exactly.” She grins back at me. We fall into a comfortable silence. The kind that doesn’t need to be filled. I can hear the faint hum of the AC, the soft rustle of her shifting next to me, the distant sound of a car passing outside. Our sleeping bags are pressed together now, the fabric crinkling every time one of us moves.\r\n“Hey,” She says after a minute. It’s only one word, but it seems heavy. \r\n“Yeah?” \r\n“If we ever stop being friends,” She says slowly, “it won’t be because of me.” I crinkle my nose, turning my head toward her.\r\n“What’s that supposed to mean?” I say, slightly offended at the implication. \r\n“Nothing,” she says quickly, like she regrets saying it.  “I just—” She shrugs. “I wouldn’t do that.” I stare at her for a second, letting my expression smooth out.\r\n“Well, I wouldn’t either,” I say. She smiles, smaller this time. I yawn, stretching my arms before letting them fall back down.\r\n“I’m actually tired now,” She mumbles. She shifts in her sleeping bag as I stare at the ceiling, trying not to overthink her comment. I know we won’t stop being friends. It's what we both wished for, after all. And if we do, well, I definitely know it won’t be because of me. That thought seems to be enough to quiet my mind. Eventually, I hear a snore that I know all too well after years of sleepovers.\r\n“Goodnight Radhu,” I say softly, looking over at her rising and falling form. I close my eyes, but I don’t move away.\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\n2026: Instaspiral\r\nThe car is too quiet. I sit there for a second with the engine off, breathing in my sweaty aura. My reflection stares back at me in the rearview mirror: flushed, damp, with strands of hair sticking to my face. I look so tired. Not in a cute, post-workout glow way. In a rat that crawled out of a sewer kind of way. I lean forward and wipe under my eyes with the heel of my palm, like that’ll fix it. Damn it, why did I let her see me like that? Panting, sweaty, and unbelievably out of shape. I couldn’t have been an easier target if I tried. I let out a sharp breath and lean back in my seat.\r\n“Relax,” I mutter. “It’s not that deep.” Except it is. Because it’s her. By the time I get home, I don’t even bother turning the lights on. I drop my keys on the counter, kick off my shoes, and head straight to the bathroom. The overhead light is harsh as I stare at myself in the mirror. My skin looks dull. My jawline’s softer than I remember. And my stomach… I turn slightly to the side. God, when did that happen? I suck in automatically, my muscle memory kicking in. Hold. Hold. Hold— I let it out. Great. \r\nI grab my phone and head back to bed, not even bothering to change out of my nasty gym garb. I don’t even think about it; I just open Instagram. Search: Radhika Sharma. Her profile comes up immediately. Picture: flawless. Hair: perfect. Makeup: effortless. It’s like she just wakes up like that. She’s so perfect it’s infuriating. Even her bio is clean, minimal, and confident in a way that makes me irrationally irritated: Enjoying life, a day at a time. \r\nIt’s not long before I’m sucked into the black hole I know all too well. I’m tapping and scrolling before I even register what’s happening. Gym selfies. Event photos. Smiling with people I don’t recognize. Smiling with people I do. And then there it is. A post from three days ago: professional photo, stage lighting, and full glam. The caption reads:\r\nMISS NORTH TEXAS PRELIMINARY FINALIST\r\nI stare at it. I swipe like I’m on autopilot. Next photo. She’s in a dress. The tight, structured kind that makes you look like you have your life together. One hand on her hip and chin slightly tilted, like a perfectly posed Barbie doll. I zoom in without meaning to. Her waist. Her posture. Her smile. Everything about her looks painstakingly curated. I can’t take it anymore. I drop my phone onto the bed like it burned me.\r\n“Okay!” I say out loud. “Whatever.” I huff. It’s just a pageant. People do those all the time. It doesn’t mean anything. I pick my phone up again. It doesn’t take long before I see him. Krish. He's tagged in a group photo from a night out. His arm is slung casually around her shoulder, like he belongs there. My chest tightens. I zoom into his smiling face. Together, they look so… easy. I remember when we were easy. I zoom back out. His other arm is around a fiercely stunning older woman. She has a stern expression and, even through the screen, exudes confidence. She’s tagged too. I tap: Tiffany Nair. Mrs. North Texas 2025. I sigh and lock my phone before tossing it on the bed.\r\nA microsecond goes by before I reach for it again. I go back to the pageant post. I reread the caption. Scroll down all the way this time. Application link in bio. I click on it. Deadline… still open. I tense for a moment. A small, ridiculous thought pops into my head. I could do that. I laugh under my breath.\r\n“Yeah. Okay.” Me? On stage? In front of judges? I can barely survive ten minutes on a StairMaster. I don’t even have the money for dresses like that. Or the body. Or- I stop. Radhika. A flash of her smug face hits me. The way she had to mention Krish. The way she had to rub her promotion in my face. Something in my chest hardens. I sit up straighter, then look back at the screen. \r\nI could do that. Why not? It’s not like she’s the only one who can. It’s not like she’s better than me. I open the form. Application for Miss North Texas. I start typing. It’s like my fingers have a mind of their own. Soon enough, I’ve filled in all the fields. I read over them once, twice, three times. I hover my finger over the submit button. Finally, I hesitate. Is this insane? I have no idea what I’m doing. I turn my phone off, and then I’m met with the reflection on the black screen. I bite the inside of my cheek. What happened to you, Sareena? I clench my jaw. \r\n“Fuck it.” I turn on my phone one last time.\r\nApplication received.\r\nLet’s do this.\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\n2019: Roses are Red\r\nIt starts small, with a sticky note tucked in between the pages of my chemistry textbook. The handwriting is dark, rough, and slightly shaky. I don’t recognize it. You looked beautiful today. No name. \r\n“Hm.” I smile to myself, wondering who this secret admirer could be. The next day, it’s a single orange rose taped awkwardly to my locker like it wasn’t fully thought through. I gently peel it off, trying to hide my smile. Radhika’s next to me, digging through her bag. \r\n“Hey,” I say. “Look at this.” \r\nShe glances over.\r\n“A flower.” She looks unimpressed.\r\n“An orange flower.” I stare at her, waiting for a bigger reaction.\r\n“So?”\r\n“Um, hello? That’s my favorite color? I think someone has a crush on me.” I can’t help but beam. She snorts in response. It stings, and my smile falters.\r\n“Or someone’s messing with you.”\r\n“Why are you so negative? It’s cute.” I frown slightly, feeling the slightest hint of irritation creeping up on me. \r\n“It’s creepy. Not the same thing as cute.” She looks down, returning her attention to her bookbag. \r\n“Don’t be salty because I’m getting attention.” The edge in my voice is unmistakable now. She exhales a mirthless laugh, almost involuntarily, not looking up at me.\r\n“You think I’m jealous? Please.” \r\n“If the shoe fits.”\r\n“Yeah, I’m deeply upset.” She concedes sarcastically. I don’t let it ruin my mood. The notes don’t stop coming. Sometimes they’re tucked between pages of my textbook. Other times they’re wedged through the vents of my locker. They’re never signed, but somehow I’ve never felt more seen before. \r\n        You should wear your hair up more.\r\n        Beautiful things don’t ask for attention.\r\n        I wish you were mine.\r\nIt becomes a habit to check my locker for them. Like, actually checking. I notice patterns too. They’re usually there by second period, same shaky, dark handwriting. And then one day, the pieces click together. It has to be him. We started chatting in first period around the same time the notes started. And I’ve never seen his handwriting before, so of course I didn’t recognize it. I even off-handedly mentioned my favorite color in class once. I can’t believe he was paying that much attention. \r\n“It’s Krish,” I say, dropping into the seat next to Radhika at lunch. \r\n“What is?” She doesn’t look up, wholly occupied with tearing her water bottle wrapper into tiny strips.\r\n“The notes?” I say. “It has to be him.” She finally glances at me. \r\n“How come?” She doesn’t stop fiddling with the wrapper.\r\n“Because he literally complimented me yesterday,” I say. “And he’s been talking to me more.”\r\n“That doesn’t mean anything.” She looks down again. \r\n“Would you stop playing with that trash and listen to me?” I laugh. “Who else would it be?” She shrugs, dropping the wrapper on the table and looking away.\r\n“It’s not just anyone,” I say, pressing through her silence. “It’s consistent. That means it’s someone I know.” \r\n“Then just ask him.” She starts to pick at her food instead, already looking bored with the conversation.\r\n“Where’s the fun in that?” I grin. She rolls her eyes. \r\nIt’s just another day in Chemistry when he turns around and gives me a single rose, grinning. His trademark move. Now there’s no doubt in my mind that he was my secret admirer all along. The notes stop shortly after that, which I don’t really question. After all, why would he need to be anonymous anymore? Funny thing, though, the rose he gave me that day was red. \r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n2026: The Vulture\r\nThe room smells like hairspray and nervous sweat. Girls stand around in clusters, already familiar with each other, familiar with this strange, sterile setting. I can’t do anything but hover around the perimeter, fiddling with the strap of my bag, trying to pretend like I belong here. \r\nThen, I see someone who actually does: Radhika. She’s already chatting with some of the girls near the mirrors, her posture perfect as her head tilts just enough to look interested. She spots me staring at her, and I look away a beat too late.\r\n“Tiffany’s here,” Someone whispers beside me. The energy of the whole room shifts and the nerves in the air become tangible. This is going to be my first time meeting her, but I’m deeply intimate with her social media at this point. Tiffany Nair: winner of last year’s Miss North Texas. Endless sponsorships. Judge on this year’s panel. Perfect hair, perfect smile, and perfect disdain. Her eyes scan the room. Not me, not me, not me, please don’t be me. They land on me. Fuck me. \r\n“You,” She says suddenly, pointing across the room. My stomach drops. For one stupid second, I glance behind me to make sure she isn’t talking to someone else. She isn’t. Every girl nearby goes still.\r\n“Stand up straight.”\r\nI freeze, then straighten too fast, inadvertently dropping my bag as my shoulders jerk back. God, just kill me now.\r\n“Not like that,” She says, already walking toward me. “Relax your shoulders. Lift your chin. No, don’t strain. God, it’s like watching someone try to be a person.” A few girls laugh. My face burns.\r\n“What’s your name?”\r\n“Sareena.”\r\n“Sareena,” She repeats, like she’s testing the name for weakness. She starts circling me like a vulture eyeing roadkill. I suddenly become aware of everything at once: the sweat collecting at the base of my spine, the way my shoulders keep tensing back up, the fact that everyone in the room is pretending not to stare while obviously staring.\r\n“Tell me, do you think you belong here?”\r\n“Um. Well,  I-” I quickly wipe my hands on my thighs, trying to get rid of the sweat on my palms. Fuck, I hope no one saw that. \r\n“No ‘um’s darling. If you don’t already know, that’s your answer.” She turns to the room. \r\n“Everyone. Do you think you belong here?”\r\n“We know we belong here,” A girl says, confidently, as chuckles erupt around the room. Tiffany smiles.\r\n“See?” She looks back at me. “Do yourself a favor and go home. Maybe next year you’ll have better luck.” The girls laugh a little louder this time. I don’t look at Radhika. I can’t. My face heats with humiliation as I grab my bag off the floor and leave. That’s when I feel someone grabbing my wrist. I turn. Of course it’s her. \r\n“Sareena, wait!” Her fingers close around my wrist before I can reach the door. The contact sends a jolt through me so sudden and familiar that it almost makes me angrier. I yank my arm away and keep walking.\r\n“What, Radhika? Are you here to rub that in my face?” I sneer, more venom slipping into my voice than I intended. She pauses, an expression I’m not expecting playing out on her face. Was that… hurt? She glances back to the room, and I can see the muscles in her jaw tense.\r\n“No, Sareena. Tiffany’s hard on everyone, especially new people. You can’t let her get to you.”\r\n“Too late for that.” I scoff, the edges of my eyes stinging with fresh tears. Suddenly, Radhika takes a cautious step closer. She hesitates for a moment before saying, \r\n“I wasn’t expecting to see you here, you know.” Her words land softer than I expect.\r\n“Yeah, I know. Apparently I don’t belong here anyways. Happy?”\r\n“Sareena, stop. I’m just surprised, is all. Never would’ve guessed beauty pageants would be your thing.” \r\nA tear escapes my eye, betraying what little composure I have left. She lifts her hand and seems to hesitate for a moment before letting it fall again. What was that? Was she about to… wipe the tear from my cheek?\r\n“Listen. I’ll help you, okay? We can work out together, eat together, and I’ll show you how to fix your posture and do your makeup.” \r\nI freeze. This was the last thing I expected from her.  \r\n“Why? Why are you helping me?” I ask her, genuinely bewildered. She thinks for a second, as if she doesn’t know the answer herself.\r\n“When you see an injured puppy on the side of the road, you can’t help but give it a home.” \r\nOuch. \r\n“I’ll see you at 6:00 am tomorrow? At the gym?” I sense an almost imperceptible waver in her question, as if she’s bracing herself for my answer.\r\n“Deal.”\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\n2020: High School Sweethearts\r\nEverything feels so easy with him. He walks me to class, texts me constantly, compliments me, and holds my hand in the hallway like it’s something he wants people to see. Girls smile at me differently now. Teachers catch us holding hands and pretend not to notice. Every time Krish kisses my cheek before class, someone nearby inevitably whistles or fake gags. It’s just another Tuesday when I notice it as I go to open my locker. A crumpled piece of paper on the floor, half shoved against the wall. I almost ignore it. But then I recognize the handwriting. My stomach flips. I crouch and pick it up, smoothing it out carefully. You looked beautiful today. It’s one of the notes. The first one. Who the fuck would do this? \r\n“What’s that?” I look up. Radhika’s standing there, watching me. I hold it up. \r\n“This was on the floor.” I want answers. She glances at it, then shrugs.\r\n “So?” The hallway buzzes around us: locker doors slamming shut, sneakers squeaking against tile, someone yelling across the corridor about practice after school.\r\n“‘So?’” I repeat. “It’s one of the notes,” I whisper hotly as a group of freshmen pass us by.\r\n “Okay, and?” she says. \r\n“And?” I stare at her. “Why is it crumpled up?” She lets out a short laugh. \r\n“I don’t know. Maybe because it’s trash?” My chest tightens. \r\n“It’s not trash.” The paper crackles in my grip. I flatten one corner carefully with my thumb, trying to smooth out the crease running through the middle of the sentence. Somehow that’s what bothers me most.\r\n“It’s literally a sticky note. Besides, you carry it around like a goddamn bible.” \r\n“Can’t you see that it’s important to me? It’s the first note that Krish gave me,” I snap. Someone walks past behind Radhika and glances between us before quickly looking away. Great, now people are noticing. Her expression changes so quickly I almost miss it. For a split second, she looks almost embarrassed. Something tightens around her mouth before she schools her face back into indifference.  \r\n“Okay, sorry. I didn’t realize it was that deep.” She meets my glare with an eyeroll.\r\n“Did you do this?” I stand up. She looks at me like I just asked something stupid.\r\n “Do what?”\r\n “Throw it on the floor,” I say. “Crumple it up.” \r\nShe scoffs. \r\n“Why would I care enough to do that?”\r\n I stare at her. Because you’re jealous. The thought lands so easily it feels like fact. \r\n“Why are you acting so weird?,” I ask. Her face screws up. \r\n “You’re the one—” She stops, then laughs. “Picking up garbage off the floor.”\r\n“It’s not garbage!” A locker slams somewhere down the hall hard enough to make me jump. The fluorescent lights overhead suddenly feel too bright. My vision blurs slightly. I blink hard before the tears can fully form.\r\n“Right, a crumpled up piece of paper isn’t garbage.” \r\nI smooth it out again, more carefully this time, my hands shaky. \r\n“Why are you acting like this?” I look at her. For a second—just a second—there’s something there. Not annoyance or jealousy. Something else. Then it’s gone. I fold the note carefully and slide it into my bag. \r\n“Whatever,” I say. “You’re just mad.” \r\n“About what?”\r\n “That someone likes me,” Around us, people keep moving through the hallway like nothing’s happening. Someone laughs nearby. A teacher calls for students to get to class. “And it’s not you.” Between us, everything suddenly feels deathly still. Regret crashes into me the second it leaves my lips. I’m about to take it back and apologize, but then she just laughs. I shut my mouth.  \r\n“Trust me,” she says, “I’m not losing sleep over your mystery boyfriend.” \r\n“Okay,” I say, my expression hardening. “Good.” \r\n“Good,” she echoes. I close my locker, turn to leave, then glance back. She’s still standing there, looking at me, her expression unreadable. I wait a microsecond too long for her to say something, anything. She doesn’t. So I turn around and don’t look back.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\n2026: Stand Up Straight\r\nThe next few weeks go by in a blur. Radhika has me waking up at the crack of dawn every morning to work out. Or more accurately, to try to keep up with her. This girl is insane, I swear. Every morning, without fail: 30 minutes on the StairMaster, an hour of weightlifting, and then a mile run. It doesn’t stop there. She throws out every guilty pleasure I have in my kitchen. Chocolate pretzels and kurekure are replaced with salads and protein shakes. \r\n“Trust me,” she says, tossing a family-size bag of chips into the trash like it personally offended her. “You won’t even miss it.” I do. Immediately. By day three, I’m fantasizing about salt. By day five, I’m googling “low calorie snacks” like it’s a personality trait. By day seven, I stop thinking about it altogether. Which somehow feels worse.\r\n“Again,” she says at the squat rack, not even out of breath. I’m halfway through a set I didn’t agree to doing.\r\n“I literally cannot feel my arms.”\r\n“That’s the point.” I glare at her through the mirror. She doesn’t even look at me, just adjusts my elbow slightly, like she’s fixing a crooked picture frame.\r\n“Form,” she says. “If you’re going to suffer, at least do it correctly.” \r\n“God, you’re annoying.” I huff out a laugh despite myself.\r\n“And you’re sloppy,” she shoots back, finally meeting my eyes in the mirror. There’s something there, something almost playful. Almost. I push through the set because now I have something to prove. Not to Tiffany. Not to the other girls. But to her.\r\nIt starts to become routine. Every day, 6:00am sharp. She’s already there when I walk in. Hair tied, outfit coordinated, like she sleeps in athleisure. I stop being late. I stop complaining. I stop thinking about stopping. At some point, it no longer feels like effort and starts feeling like necessity. Like if I skip one day, everything I’ve built will collapse. Like I’ll go back to being the girl Tiffany laughed at. The first time Radhika compliments me, I almost miss it.\r\n“Your posture’s better,” she says casually, stretching her legs in the sauna. It takes me a second to process.\r\n“That’s it?” I ask. “No emotional speech?” She rolls her eyes.\r\n“Do you want one?”\r\nI shrug, trying to play it off.\r\n“No.” A beat.\r\n“You’re doing good,” she adds, quieter this time. Something in my chest shifts. It’s small. But it’s there. I hate that it matters. I hate that she’s the one saying it. I hate that I want her to say it again. \r\nWe tackle makeup next. In her apartment, she sits me down in front of the mirror like I’m a project.\r\n“This is wrong,” she says, wiping half my face off without warning. “Your color corrector isn’t even close to being the right shade.”\r\n“Okay, rude.”\r\n“Okay, true.” she retorts.\r\nShe leans in closer, blending with quick, practiced movements. Her fingers brush my cheek, my jaw, my temple. I go still without meaning to.\r\n“Look up,” she murmurs. I do, looking up at her face. She’s deep in concentration, pursing her lips the way she does when she’s focused. Her face is closer than I expect. For a second, neither of us moves. I can feel the warmth of her breath on my nose. Man, even her breath smells good. Then she pulls back.\r\n“Better,” she says, like nothing happened. I look at myself in the mirror. It’s different. Sharper. More like someone who belongs in her world.\r\n“See?” she says. “Not that hard.” \r\nWith my makeup finally up to Radhika's standards, there's only one major obstacle left in my transformation: my wardrobe, which terrifies me. Looking like a beauty queen requires a beauty queen's budget, after all. Radhika, however, has other ideas.\r\n“Looking expensive and being expensive are two completely different things,” she tells me, dragging me into a thrift store. She starts rifling through the racks like she's searching for buried treasure.\r\n“Thrifting is an art, Reena. You'd be amazed what designer labels people throw away. Half the girls in pageantry spend thousands trying to look rich when all you really need is a good eye, a decent tailor, and the patience to dig through some bins.” She pulls out a gorgeous satin evening gown and checks the tag.\r\n“See? Designer. Twelve dollars.” I stare at her.\r\n“You're telling me I've been financially intimidated by a bunch of girls wearing secondhand clothes?” Radhika grins.\r\n“Welcome to the beauty industry, sweetheart.”\r\nWhen I walk back into the room for the next session, it’s quieter than before. Not silent. Just aware. Heads turn. Not all of them. But enough. Tiffany looks up. Her eyes land on me again. This time, I don’t look away.\r\n“Oh,” she says, taking me in. “You came back.”\r\n“I did.” A pause. She hums, unimpressed. Or pretending to be.\r\n“We’ll see how long that lasts.”\r\n“Oh it will last. I’m not going anywhere.” The bite in my tone surprises me, and some girls glance over. Good, let them. I double down, narrowing my eyes. I didn’t do all that goddamn work for nothing. Tiffany surveys me. There’s an imperceptible smirk underneath her harsh expression. \r\n“Hm. About time you grew a backbone.” And that’s it. She doesn’t laugh. She doesn’t tear me apart. She moves on. And that feels like a win.\r\n“Nice work, Reena,” I hear Radhika say beside me. I beam at her, grateful. I almost thank her. Almost. But then I remember Krish. The way she said his name. The way it sounded so easy coming out of her mouth. She almost had me fooled for a second. \r\n“What’s wrong?” She asks, noticing the drop in my expression. \r\n“Thanks for your help, Radhika. Good luck on Show Day.” The words come out polite. Practiced. Not meant for her. She opens her mouth to say something, but closes it instead. Good. I turn away before she can try again, before she can fix it and make me forget why I’m here in the first place. Because she almost did. And that’s the most dangerous part.\r\n________________\r\n2020: What Goes on Behind Closed Doors\r\nThe hallway is almost empty by the time I head to my locker. It feels almost eerie after hours. The lights hum overhead, flickering just slightly, and my footsteps echo louder than they should. I’m halfway through texting Krish something stupid about forgetting my notebook when I hear his voice. It’s coming from behind a closed classroom door. I stop. It’s automatic. But then I hear her. Something tightens in my chest, sharp and immediate. I tell myself it’s nothing. They probably just ran into each other. That happens. We all go to the same school. It’s not—\r\n“This is a bad idea,” Radhika is saying. I inch closer to the door, desperate to eavesdrop. Her voice is low. Careful. Not the clipped, irritated version of her I’ve been getting lately.\r\n“I know,” Krish replies. “But you’re the one who keeps brushing it off.”\r\n“I’m not brushing it off,” she says. “I was trying to help you.” Help him? I take a step closer, pressing myself against the door, just enough to see them through the narrow gap in the window. They’re standing too close. Not accidentally. Not like people who just ran into each other. Like people who are trying to guard a secret.\r\n“You asked me how to ask her out,” Radhika continues. “I helped you. That’s it.”\r\n“I know what you told me,” he says. “I’m saying it’s not just that.” My stomach dips.\r\n“What does that even mean?” she asks, sharper now.\r\n“It means you don’t just give someone that much attention for no reason,” he says. “You can’t just look at me like that.”\r\n“I’m not—” she cuts herself off, exhaling. “You’re twisting this into something it’s not.”\r\n“Am I?” he says. There’s a pause. A long one. The kind that fills the space with something thick and wrong.\r\n“Why are we still talking about this?” she says.\r\n“Because it didn’t stop there,” he says. My grip on my phone tightens. The world starts spinning around me.\r\n“It shouldn’t have happened,” she says quickly. There’s something in her voice now I’ve never heard before. Not annoyance. Not sarcasm. Panic. It’s deeply unsettling.\r\n“Okay,” he says. “But it did.” My heart pounds.\r\n“It was a mistake,” she continues. “Let it be one.”\r\n“A mistake?” he repeats. “That’s what you’re calling it now?”\r\n“I told you, I regret it,” she says, her voice breaking just slightly. “I shouldn’t have—I don’t even know why I—” She cuts herself off. Too late. My ears ring. I feel like I’m standing outside my own body, like I’m watching this happen to someone else.\r\n“What, you’re just going to pretend it didn’t happen?” Krish says. My chest tightens so hard it almost hurts. I can’t take it anymore. I don’t even think before I burst open the door.\r\n“What the hell is going on?” My voice cuts through the classroom, louder than I mean for it to be. They both turn. Radhika’s face goes white. Krish just looks caught.\r\n“Say it,” I say, because suddenly I need to hear it out loud. “What is he talking about?”\r\n“Sareena, it didn’t mean anything,” she says finally. That’s the worst possible thing she could’ve said. I let out a short, disbelieving laugh.\r\n“Didn’t mean anything?” I repeat. “You—” I gesture between them, my hands shaking now. “You kissed him and it didn’t mean anything?” She flinches. Which is all the confirmation I need.\r\n“Just let me explain” she says again, softer now. “I didn’t–”\r\n“No,” I say, shaking my head. “No, I’m done listening to you.” Because what is there to listen to? That she didn’t mean to? That she was trying to help? That it just happened?\r\n“You helped him,” I say, the words tasting bitter in my mouth. “Helped him ask me out.” Her eyes flicker.\r\n“Yes,” she says.\r\n“And this is what you have to show for help?” I laugh again, harsher this time. “This is your version of being a good friend?”\r\n“Sareena, just listen to me–” she says.\r\n“You let it happen.” I say. \r\nShe opens her mouth like she’s about to say something—something real this time—but I don’t give her the chance. Because I don’t want to hear it. Because whatever it is, it won’t fix this.\r\n“You’ve always been like this,” I say. She goes still.\r\n“Like what?” she asks. I meet her eyes.\r\n“Jealous.” The word lands heavy between us. Final. She looks like I just hit her. I don’t stay to see what she does next. I turn and walk away, my chest tight, my vision blurred, the sound of my own footsteps too loud in my ears. Behind me, neither of them follows. And somehow that’s the part that hurts the most.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n2026: And the Winner is\r\nBackstage is chaos. Cosmetic fumes hang thick in the air, mixing with perfume and nerves as the sharp heat of stage lights bleed through the curtains. Girls rush past in robes and half-done makeup, heels clicking, voices overlapping. I sit in front of the mirror, trying to keep my hands steady.\r\n“Hold still.” Radhika’s voice cuts through my thoughts. I snap out of my rumination as her hands enter my field of view. I don’t turn, but I don’t stop her either. Her fingers slip into my hair, deft and practiced, pinning a loose strand into place. She’s closer than she needs to be. I can feel the warmth of her breath near my ear, the light brush of her knuckles against my neck.\r\n“Your part shifted,” she murmurs. “You’d get eaten alive under the lights like that.”\r\n“Good thing I have you,” I say, the words coming out flatter than I mean for them to. A pause. Her hands still for just a second before continuing.\r\n“Yeah,” she says quietly. “Good thing.” I catch her eyes in the mirror. For a moment, it’s just us. I forget we’re even at a pageant for a moment. Then.\r\n“Sareena.”\r\nHis voice cuts through the area like a knife. I freeze. Radhika’s hands stop in my hair. I don’t need to turn to know he’s there. Of course he is. Of course he chose now. I stand too quickly, chair scraping against the floor.\r\n“Can we talk?” Krish asks. \r\n“Timing’s amazing, as always.” I laugh under my breath.\r\n Radhika steps back, putting space between us. I can feel it immediately.\r\n“Go ahead,” she says, but her voice is tight. \r\n“Actually,” I say, turning back to Krish, “I’m kind of busy right now. You see the situation?” He looks between us, something unreadable flickering across his face.\r\n“I just wanted to wish you good luck,” he says. I nod slowly.\r\n“Wow. That almost sounds sincere.”\r\n“Sareena.”\r\n“I said I’m busy,” I cut him off. He hesitates, like he wants to push further. \r\nI interrupt whatever idiotic thought he's forming. \r\n“Why are you here anyway? You don’t belong backstage.”\r\nHe blinks, a bit thrown off. \r\n“Sareena, I’m working the event. My aunt’s running it. Tiffany Nair?”\r\n“What? You’re her spawn?”\r\n“Well, her brother’s spawn, yeah.”\r\nOf course. Of course he’s here. Of course he has a reason to be here. That shouldn’t make it better. It doesn’t.\r\n“Great,” I mutter. “Nepotism. Love that for you.”\r\n“Sareena… I’m not here because of—whatever you think this is.” He exhales, running a hand through his hair.\r\n“Oh, I think I know exactly what this is.”\r\n“No, you don’t,” he says, sharper now. “You never did.”\r\n“I already told you,” he continues, quieter. “What happened back then—it was a mistake. I shouldn’t have pushed. I shouldn’t have—”\r\n“Stop.” My voice cuts him off cold.\r\n“Sareena, if you would stop being stubborn and just listen for a minute–”\r\n“Wow, I’m stubborn.” I laugh incredulously.\r\n“Break a leg, you two.”\r\nThe music swells. We line up for the opening number. All of a sudden lights hit my face, blinding and hot, and I’m not backstage anymore. I’m smiling, walking, turning. Every movement is deliberate and practiced. On the outside, I’m composed. But inside, everything is turned up too loud. Every glance feels loaded. Every step feels like it’s balancing on something fragile. I catch sight of her across the stage. She moves with effortless grace and composure. Like she always has. Like none of this is touching her at all. Liar. Before I can process it, we’re back behind the curtains. The second we’re offstage, she’s in front of me.\r\n“Sareena, listen to me.” \r\n“I don’t want to.” I keep walking.\r\n“Too bad,” she says, grabbing my arm. I spin around to face her.\r\n“Let go.” \r\nShe does. But she doesn’t step back.\r\n“You don’t get to shut me out like this,” she says.\r\n“I don’t get to?” I laugh. “That’s rich.”\r\n“You think I wanted any of that?” she snaps.\r\n“Oh, I don’t know,” I shoot back. “You seemed pretty comfortable.”\r\n“I never wanted him, Sareena.” \r\nThe words hit harder than I expect.\r\n“Wow,” I say. “That’s supposed to make me feel better?”\r\n“I’m serious,” she says, stepping closer. “It was never about him.”\r\n“Then what was it about?” I demand. Her jaw tightens.\r\n“Are you that oblivious?” Something in her tone makes my stomach drop.\r\n“What do you mean?” I ask. She lets out a sharp breath, taking a second to compose herself. She looks down at her feet, before looking back up to meet my eyes.\r\n“Krish and I aren’t together. This is the first time we’ve spoken since high school. I swear it was a coincidence that he’s Tiffany’s nephew.” The world tilts.\r\n“What?”\r\n“You think I care about Krish?” she says, her voice shaking now. “You think I’d risk everything for him?” I stare at her. Nothing makes sense.\r\n“Then why?” I ask. “Why did you do it?”\r\n“Because he asked me for help,” she says, her voice cracking. The words start spilling out. “And he kept—he kept pushing, and I—” She cuts herself off, swallowing hard.\r\n“He kissed me. I pushed him away, and then you walked in,” she says. “It was never about him.” She looks at me, her expression pleading. My head starts spinning.\r\n“This doesn’t make any sense,” I say.\r\n“It does,” she says desperately. “If you just—”\r\n“What about the notes?” I cut in. She freezes.\r\n“What?”\r\n“The notes,” I repeat. “The ones in my locker. Why would you destroy them?” Her face changes. She looks down once more, taking a deep breath.\r\n“They were from me, Sareena” she says quietly. The words don’t register at first. But then everything starts to slowly click into place.\r\n“What?”\r\n“I wrote them,” she says. “All of them.” My chest tightens.\r\n“No,” I say immediately. “No, that doesn’t—”\r\n“You thought it was him,” she continues, her voice breaking. “And I couldn’t— I couldn’t stand it anymore.” The hallway. The crumpled note. Her face. Oh. Oh.\r\n“I…” The words catch in my throat. “I didn’t know.” \r\n“I know,” she says.\r\n“And I—” I shake my head. \r\n“I thought you— I thought you were—”\r\n“Jealous?” she finishes. I don’t answer. Because I did. Because I said it. Because I meant it.\r\n“I didn’t know,” I say again, weaker this time.\r\n“I know,” she repeats.\r\n“Radhika, I-” I start.\r\n“Sareena, Radhika, you’re on in 10!” The announcer’s voice cuts through everything. We both turn. Just like that, it’s over.\r\nThe evening gown segment feels like a dream. We walk. Pose. Turn. Smile. Like nothing just happened. But every time I catch her eye, something shifts. I feel something that I never have before. Something I don’t have a name for yet. Soon enough, the Q&A portion of the pageant starts.\r\nIt’s her turn first.\r\n“What does beauty mean to you?” Radhika smiles, but it’s not the polished pageant smile. It’s softer. Real. \r\n“Some people say beauty is power, or social currency. But I think that real beauty is quiet,” she says. “It’s not something that tries to prove itself.” A pause.\r\n“Beautiful things don’t ask for attention.” My breath catches.\r\n“They just… exist. And if you’re lucky enough to notice them, that’s enough.” Her eyes find mine.\r\n“And sometimes,” she adds, “the most beautiful things are the ones that go unseen.”\r\n“Contestant 12, what is a moment of growth that defines you?” My turn. I step forward, mic in hand. The lights are blinding. But for once, I don’t feel small.\r\n“I think…” I start, my voice steadier than I expect, “growth happens when you’re able to look back at a situation and see it from a different perspective..” A murmur ripples through the audience. I don’t look at them. I don’t look at her, but I know she’s looking at me.\r\n“At some point, you have to decide whether you’re going to keep believing the story you told yourself… or accept that you might have misunderstood it completely.” My throat tightens.\r\n“But I think real growth is choosing to see things clearly, and give each side of the story the weight it deserves. To assume good faith, even when it’s uncomfortable.” Even when it hurts. Even when it changes everything.\r\n\r\n\r\n…\r\n“And for Second Runner Up, we have.. Radhika Sharma!” Applause erupts. Cameras flash. Radhika steps forward, composed as ever, accepting her tiara like she was always meant to wear it.\r\n“And for First Runner Up, everyone congratulate…  Sareena Rao!” The rest blurs. I don’t process the lights, the tiara, the sash. It all gets done to me like a Nascar pit crew. I smile when I’m supposed to. Turn when I’m told. But I don’t hear the crowd.\r\nAll I hear is her. Her voice. Her answer.\r\nBeautiful things don’t ask for attention.\r\nHow could I have missed it? Suddenly I’m backstage again. It’s quieter now. Back to reality. She’s already walking toward me.\r\n“Congrats, Miss Runner Up,” she says softly, almost teasing.\r\n“Radhika.” My voice is steadier than I feel. She stops.\r\nFor a second, neither of us moves. And then it clicks, cleaner than anything has all night. All this time, I thought she was trying to take something from me. I never realized she was trying to give something. And I was the one who turned away. Before I can retreat into the version of the story I’ve been telling myself for years, I close the distance between us.\r\nI kiss her. It’s messy. Sudden. Not planned. Just real. She freezes, just long enough for panic to flicker, and then she kisses me back.\r\nLike she’s been waiting. Like she always has. When we pull apart, the world feels smaller. Quieter. Like it’s finally settled into place.\r\n“Radhu,” I whisper.\r\nThe name feels different now. Softer. Like something I almost lost. Her forehead rests against mine, her breath uneven. \r\n“You’re so late,” she murmurs, half a laugh, half something else. I let out a breath I didn’t realize I was holding. \r\n“I know.” I look at her, and she holds my gaze. The stage lights catch on her hair, and her umber eyes glint. Beautiful things don’t ask for attention.\r\n“I see you now.”"}, {"id": "story-two", "title": "Tigerlily", "text": "Tigerlily\r\nNalini Kantheti\r\n\r\n\r\nLast Boat\r\nI slam my cabin door shut as I storm into my room. I gulp, trying to swallow the lump in my throat. I beeline to the mirror and stare. Fix your hair. It looks like a rat’s nest. My mother’s voice echoes in my mind. My eyes wander to the frizzy bangs I cut myself. I gently brush them with my fingers. They naturally start towards the emerging pimple on my left cheek. We need to start putting you on Accutane. That is getting plain disgusting to look at. I start to pop it, then freeze, before collapsing backwards onto the bed. I slam my hand down on the pillow next to me, grip it until my knuckles turn white, and then bring it to my mouth as I let out what would normally be a blood curdling scream. \r\nWe’re literally on vacation and she still has something to say. Mother has an exceptional talent for reversing any good mood you might find yourself in. \r\nI inhale deeply, finally feeling myself calm down. It’s okay, Mandy. You are enough, just the way you are. I look up at the ceiling lights and watch them slowly blur. I slowly close my eyes, embracing the wetness on my temples. Eventually I feel it traveling farther, down to my cheeks, circling my ears, splashing my toes. Wait, my toes? I look down, and see water coating the floor. Huh? Suddenly, the room is red. The emergency light starts wailing and flashing, and my stomach turns. I rush out into the hallway which, sure enough, is steadily flooding with water. Oh, fuck. I’m on a sinking ship. \r\n The water level just keeps rising, and it’s getting harder and harder to walk to the stairs. The hallway flips sideways so suddenly my shoulder slams into the wall. I don’t realize I’m underwater until my lungs start burning. I cough up seawater as I gasp for air. I finally make it, each step increasing with heaviness and desperation. I bolt up the stairs and am met with sheer chaos. A freak storm is roaring overhead, lightning flashing dangerously close to the boat. Rain is pouring down in thick sheets, drowning out the screams. I look around, trying to locate my family. I can’t find them. Cruise staff are unloading people onto lifeboats. Fuck. Fuck. Fuck. I need to find my family. \r\n“Last boat!” The staff member calls out, looking directly at me. He then steps on himself. I rush over to him.\r\n“Sir, I can’t find my family.” I cry. He just looks at me. \r\n“Better haul ass then. You have 2 minutes.” He says as if he’s announcing boarding times on a plane. I stare at him incredulously for a moment, and then decide it’s not even worth replying. I bolt to the stairwell I came from, desperate for someone familiar. It’s now filled to the brim from the rain. I’m gonna have to dive. I yank my hair into a ponytail and lower myself into the water. It’s pitch black save for the flashing red lights. Halfway down the stairs, I can barely make out the rooms. I kick upward and slam directly into the ceiling. Ouch. I let myself sink lower and feel the walls for doorframes. \r\nMy mom’s room is the closest. She was sharing a room with my brother Kevin. And Dad. Basically everyone but me. Great parenting, if you ask me. I use all my strength to yank open the door. Nothing. I yank again, my lungs now screaming for oxygen. It’s unusually strong… Wait.  The outside water pressure. It must be pushing it shut. It’s gonna take inhuman strength to open it this way. Fuck. I start to panic when I realize I’m running out of air and need to regroup. I’m slammed wall to wall as I swim back out to the stairwell and re-enter the deck. Of course those assholes already left. Come on, I’ve seen too many lost at sea videos at this point.\r\n Of course, emergency life rafts! Not as ideal as a lifeboat, but it’ll do. I search the above board crew quarters and find 3 stocked with supplies: flare gun, rope, rations, first aid kit. Perfect. I move the supplies of all three rafts onto one. My parents are still trapped, though. \r\nFuck, think. Then I remember, the porthole that looks outside their room is just big enough to fit a person through. I have to shatter it from the outside. How the fuck am I gonna do that? I move as quickly as my panic ridden body will let me, inflating the raft with trembling fingers and lowering myself off the ship using the pulley ropes. I circle the ship, looking for the porthole to my family’s room. Bet they regret excluding me now, huh? Eventually, I see my mother’s face pushed up against a window. I think this is the longest we’ve gone without her criticizing me. She sees me on the raft outside and I see her mouth form a scream. \r\n “I see you, Mom.” I row towards the porthole and grab the butt of the flare gun, and slam it against the glass. Nothing. Luckily the porthole is just above sea-level, so I don’t have to worry about water pressure differences this time. I slam the window again.\r\n“Come on,” I sob. “Come on.” It cracks. I hit it again, harder. Suddenly, I see my mom gear up to hit it from the inside with the emergency hammer. I quickly row away, until she clears out the glass. I pull her through the porthole, and she doesn’t object. Her nails dig into my arm hard enough to hurt as I tug her through the window. She coughs violently against the raft floor, seawater pouring from her mouth. Then suddenly her hands grab my face.\r\n“Thank God,” she gasps. Just that. Not Mandy. Not criticism. Not disappointment. Thank God. Her thumbs tremble against my cheeks like she can’t believe I’m real. And for one stupid horrible second, I feel six years old again.\r\n“Get Kevin. Now.” She commands. There’s our Regina. The illusion shatters before my eyes. I look through the porthole. Water rushes past overturned furniture. Kevin’s nowhere to be seen. My lungs are still burning. My mother’s fingers tighten around my wrist.\r\n“What’s wrong with you?” My mom demands, looking angrier than ever. I almost jump into the water when the ship suddenly groans. Metal screams from every direction as the ship towers over us. It’s almost like it’s getting bigger. Wait. That’s because it’s getting closer. The ship is about to fall on us. Just as it starts turning sideways, I grab the oar and row for my life. I throw the other oar at my mother. \r\n        “Row! We’re gonna die!” She frantically starts to row… backwards.\r\n“Goddamnit Mom, other way!” She eyes me angrily in response as I muster all the adrenaline I can and push through the water. We barely make it enough distance from the ship before the porthole disappears into the water below. I see movement in the window. Could’ve been a hand. Or debris. Then it’s swallowed by blackness.The hull of the ship crashes violently into the water, sending a huge wave our way.\r\n“Hold on!” I shout as I grip the sides of the raft and brace myself. The raft is thrown sideways as the wave hits it and I watch helplessly as the food rations fly off the boat. Well, if I’m gonna die out here at least it’ll be slow and painful. \r\n“No!” My mother wails. “My baby!” She collapses on the raft in tears.\r\n“Why couldn’t it have been you?” She seethes, glaring up at me. I can’t do anything but look back at her. \r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nTulips Over Tigerlilies\r\nI’m nine years old when my mother briefly pretends we’re a normal family. The three of us kneel in the backyard garden with little plastic shovels while Dad watches football inside through the kitchen window. Technically he’s “supervising.” Realistically, he hasn’t looked up once. Kevin’s wearing Mom’s gardening gloves even though they’re too big for him. He keeps wiggling his fingers dramatically every time dirt gets on them like he’s performing surgery instead of planting bulbs in suburban Texas.        \r\n“I wanna plant tulips,” he announces.        \r\n“Of course you do,” I mutter.        \r\n“What’s that supposed to mean?” Kevin snaps immediately.        \r\n“Nothing.” I say quickly under my breath. \r\n“Mandy, don’t start.” Mom sighs without looking up from the flower packets.\r\nI didn’t even say anything. Incredible. I stare down at the packet in my lap. Bright orange tiger lilies bloom across the front.\r\n“They’re pretty,” I say quietly. My mother glances at the package.\r\n“Hm.” She doesn’t look away. I brace myself for: Too bright. Too messy. Too dramatic. Too expensive. Too weird. Instead, to my genuine shock, she says:\r\n“Well. I suppose we have room for both.” I blink at her. Kevin blinks at her too, honestly looking more offended than I am. For one weird, fragile second, I feel happy. Like actually happy. We spend the next few minutes digging holes in the dirt while Mom lectures us about spacing and root depth like she’s hosting a gardening show nobody asked for.\r\n “I need to use the bathroom,” she says, standing up. “Do not make a mess while I’m gone.” The sliding door shuts behind her. Silence. Kevin looks over at my flowers. Then at me. And smiles. Oh no. Before I can react, he stomps directly onto the patch I just planted. They crunch sickeningly. The bulbs flatten beneath his sneakers.\r\n“What the hell?” I shove him hard enough to stumble backward. Kevin bursts into laughter.\r\n“They looked stupid anyway.” \r\n“You asshole!” He stomps again. And again. Orange petals and dirt scatter everywhere.\r\n“Stop!” I lunge toward him, but Kevin retaliates. He grabs my wrists easily and shoves me backward into the dirt.\r\n“Moooom!” he suddenly screams. Oh, you little psychopath. The sliding door flies open.\r\n“What happened?” Mom demands. Kevin points at the crushed flowers.\r\n“Mandy ruined the garden!”\r\n“What?” I stare at him. “You stomped on them!” My mother turns toward me with immediate exhaustion, like I’m a customer service issue she was hoping to avoid today.\r\n“Mandy…”\r\n“No! He did it on purpose!” I point at Kevin, who’s already crying now. Fake crying, obviously. No tears. Just noises.\r\n“She pushed me!” He wails. Mom pinches the bridge of her nose.\r\n“Why are you always fighting with your brother?”\r\n“I’m not fighting with him, he literally—” I notice her see the flower packet in the dirt. Tiger lilies. \r\n“Oh.” Her expression shifts instantly. “Well, they were ugly anyway.” I freeze.\r\n“And they’re poisonous to cats,” she continues casually. “What if Mushu got outside and ate them? You’re okay with innocent blood on your hands?” I stare at her. I genuinely don’t know how to respond to that. Beside me, Kevin snorts.\r\n“Yeah,” he says. “Mandy would totally kill Mushu. She’s a freak.” Mom says nothing. The backyard suddenly feels too quiet. Kevin smirks at me through dirt-stained gloves. My mother picks up the crushed tiger lily packet and drops it into the trash.\r\nDinner that night is duck sausage and rice. Dad talks about mortgage rates, and mom talks about Kevin’s math placement exam. Nobody talks about the garden. I keep waiting for her to look at me. To say, I know it wasn’t your fault. Instead she asks me to pass the chili oil without making eye contact. By the time I get upstairs, dirt still cakes beneath my fingernails. I sit cross-legged on my bedroom floor and scrape it out silently with a mechanical pencil. Tiny black crescents collect beside my knee. I don’t realize I’m crying until one of the tears turns the dirt to mud. Stupid. They were just flowers. I wipe my face angrily and crawl into bed without changing clothes. \r\nHours later, I get up to use the bathroom. And freeze. Outside my bedroom door sits a single tiger lily bulb. A surviving one. Dirt smears across the carpet around it like someone carried it upstairs in a clenched fist. I stare down the hallway. It’s dark and quiet. Kevin’s door is shut. Mom and Dad’s room is dark too. No note. No explanation. I pick the bulb up carefully in both hands. For one stupid hopeful second, I imagine Mom coming upstairs after dinner. Quietly digging through the trash once nobody was looking. But the bulb is dirty, and Mom hates dirt. Which means– I look toward Kevin’s room. But I just hear silence. I never ask him about it, and he never brings it up either.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nTeach a Man to Fish\r\nIt’s day 2 when she finally snaps out of it. While my mother was curled up, crying and throwing up nonstop, I actually managed to catch a mackerel. There were some fish hooks and twine in the raft, and by some miracle this fish was stupid enough to get caught without any bait. I’m in the middle of filleting it when she finally says something not about Kevin. \r\n“Mandy. Where did you get that?” She asks, a needlessly accusatory edge to her voice.\r\n“Um. I caught it. We have to eat.” I gauge her reaction. She looks at me incredulously. \r\n“Stop lying. I know you wouldn’t be able to catch anything.” I return her incredulous gaze. I wait for her to say she’s joking, anything that would restore my faith in her humanity. She doesn’t. \r\n“You’re not serious, are you?” I say\r\n“Don’t you dare take that tone with me. I am your mother. Let’s not forget who’s in charge here.” My face goes blank. There’s no way. Even after all of this, after I caught us food, after I saved her life, she doesn’t respect me. I want to fume, to scream, to shake her by her shoulders. But I don’t do any of that.\r\n“You’re right, Mom. I’m sorry.”\r\n“That’s what I thought.” She still doesn’t get it, does she? I turn away and begin to eat my mackerel. Raw unseasoned fish has never tasted so good.\r\n“Hello, Missy. Where’s my share?” Mom cuts in.\r\n“Oh.” I look down at the rest of the fish in my hands. Then I turn towards her and toss the rest of the mackerel overboard. \r\n“What the hell is wrong with you?” She screeches and lunges toward the end of the raft but she’s too late. She looks more angry than I’ve even seen her before.\r\n“Oh, I’m sorry, Mom. You’re in charge here, right? So you can do your own fishing and freshwater collection.” For a moment, sheer terror flashes across her face as she looks at me. Then it curdles into raw hatred. Funny, for the first time in my life, she’s scared of me instead of disappointed. I’m not sure which I prefer. \r\n“You’d let your own mother starve?” She’s obviously trying to guilt trip me, but there’s something else there. Genuine desperation. For the first time in my entire life, she’s at my mercy. \r\n“No, Mom. I’d let you fish. Didn’t you always say coddling builds poor character?” She just stares at me. Not angry anymore. Calculating. Then, without another word, she snatches the remaining fishing line from beside me and begins fumbling with the hook. I almost laugh.        \r\n“Do you even know how to use that?”\r\n“I am not going to sit here and let my own daughter disrespect me.” Ah. So this is about dignity now. Interesting time to discover that. She jabs the hook through the line backwards. I watch silently for a moment before fixing it for her with one quick motion. Regina immediately jerks the rod away from me afterward like accepting help from me physically pains her. The raft drifts quietly for nearly twenty minutes. Then suddenly—\r\n“Ah!” my mother yelps. The line jerks violently in her hands. Against all odds, she actually caught something. A round, spotted fish bobs at the surface beside the raft. I freeze. No way. My mother beams triumphantly as she hauls it onto the raft.\r\n“Oh my God,” she laughs breathlessly. “See? It’s not that difficult.” The fish inflates instinctively, tiny spikes protruding from its body. My stomach drops.\r\n“Mom.”\r\n“What?”\r\n“Don’t touch that.” \r\nShe rolls her eyes immediately. \r\n“Mandy, it’s a fish.”\r\n“It’s a pufferfish.”\r\n“So?”\r\n“So it can kill you.” That finally gets her attention. Regina looks down at the fish\r\nuncertainly.\r\n“Is it poisonous?”\r\n“It contains tetrodotoxin.”\r\n“The tetra-what?”\r\n“A neurotoxin. Paralysis. Respiratory failure. No antidote.” She stares at me. I stare back. For the first time in my life, my mother looks at me like I know something she doesn’t. The realization seems to physically disturb her.\r\n“Well,” she says stiffly, “how was I supposed to know that?” I shrug.\r\n“I don’t know. Maybe from all those marine biology books you kept calling useless.”\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nTherapy Session\r\n“Jesus Christ, Mandy. Have you even brushed your hair today?” Ah. Home sweet home. Mom is standing in the kitchen, inspecting me like a piece of produce that's gone bad at the grocery store. I instinctively reach for my hair.\r\n“Yes, Mom.”\r\n“Then why does it look like that?”\r\n“Genetics, probably.” She gives me a look.\r\n“Don't be smart with me. Go upstairs and fix yourself. You look homeless.” I briefly consider explaining that homeless people also have access to hairbrushes, but decide against it. I'm already on thin ice for existing. \r\nI drag myself upstairs and slam my bedroom door. Not hard enough to get yelled at, obviously. Just hard enough to express my dissatisfaction with the current political regime.\r\nAnd there he is. Mango. My beautiful, perfect, orange little angel is sprawled across my pillow, blissfully unaware of the psychological warfare being conducted downstairs. His little paws are tucked beneath his chest, and his eyes are half-closed. Finally. Someone who loves me.\r\n“Hi, baby,” I whisper. Mango opens one eye.\r\n“Oh my God, you have no idea how bad my day was.” I drop my backpack onto the floor and crawl onto the bed beside him. He blinks slowly at me.\r\n“First of all, Mom thinks I'm disgusting. Shocking development, I know. And Kevin has apparently decided that my entire existence is a personal inconvenience to him. Also, I'm pretty sure everyone at school hates me.” Mango yawns.\r\n“Wow. Okay. Rude.” I laugh a little and scratch behind his ears.\r\n“You're supposed to be my therapist, you know. At least pretend to care.” He begins purring. Oh, thank God. Finally, some emotional validation.\r\n“See? This is why you're my favorite person.” I lie down beside him and rest my forehead gently against his warm little body. For a moment, everything is quiet. No Mom. No Kevin. No criticism. Just the soft vibration of his purring against my cheek. And then, for absolutely no reason, I start crying. Not cute crying, either. Ugly, pathetic, congested crying. The kind where your nose starts running and you can't breathe properly and suddenly you're eight years old again.\r\n“I don't understand,” I whisper. Mango continues purring.\r\n“I try so hard. I really do.” My voice cracks.\r\n“Why does everyone hate me?” I wrap my arms loosely around him, desperate for something warm to hold onto. Mango stops purring.\r\n“I'm sorry,” I whisper. “I just need you right now.” He squirms.\r\n“Wait. No, don't go.” Mango wriggles free from my arms, hops off the bed, and begins licking his paw on the carpet. I stare at him. Seriously?\r\n“Mango.” He continues grooming himself.\r\n“Come here, baby.” Nothing. I pat the mattress beside me.\r\n“Come on. Please?” He looks up briefly, considers my offer, and returns to licking his foot. Fantastic. Even my cat has boundaries. I sit up and wipe my face with my sleeve.\r\n“You don't like me either?” Mango flicks his tail. Something about that makes me cry harder. Which is ridiculous, obviously. He's a cat. He doesn't understand abandonment or emotional neglect or the fact that I am currently having the worst day of my life. But still. I get off the bed and sit cross-legged beside him.\r\n“Please,” I whisper, reaching toward him. “Just come sit with me.” Mango ducks beneath my hand and trots toward the door. I freeze.\r\n“Are you serious?” He stops in the doorway and looks back at me.\r\n“I'm literally begging you.” He meows once. Then leaves. I sit there for a long time, staring at the empty doorway. My bedroom feels suddenly enormous. And quiet. Too quiet.\r\nThe worst part is that I wasn't even asking for much. I didn't need him to fix anything. I didn't need advice or reassurance or a carefully structured treatment plan. I just wanted him to stay. Apparently, that's too much to ask. I look down at the orange fur clinging to my sweater. A few strands stick to the wet patches where I'd been crying. \r\nStupid cat. I pull one of the hairs off my sleeve and roll it between my fingers. It's not like he has anything better to do. He sleeps sixteen hours a day, eats food I give him, and shits in a box that I clean. The least he could do is sit still for five fucking minutes. I stare at the doorway. Then I wipe my face, get up, and close it. Whatever.\r\nI didn't want company anyway.\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCatch and Release\r\nThe ocean is eerily calm the morning Kevin comes back. Which honestly should’ve been my first warning sign. My mother sits beneath the emergency blanket, squinting weakly at the horizon while I untangle fishing wire beside her. She’s finally stopped crying every fifteen minutes, which has done wonders for morale. I glance at the sky. Thin cirrus clouds. Decent visibility. Wind heading east. Good.\r\n“We’re drifting too slowly,” I mutter.\r\n“What?”\r\n“Nothing.” Regina watches me quietly for a moment. Not glaring. Not criticizing. Just… watching. It’s weird enough to make me uncomfortable.\r\n“You think someone’s looking for us?” she asks eventually. I shrug.\r\n“Statistically? Probably.”\r\n“And realistically?” I tie another knot in the fishing line.\r\n“We’re not dead yet.” She nods slowly. For a second, it almost feels like we’re having a normal conversation. Like maybe near-death experiences and prolonged dehydration finally unlocked maternal instinct. Then she freezes.\r\n“Mandy.” Her voice cracks. I look up. Something bobs in the distance. I squint. At first I think it’s debris. Then it moves. An arm lifts weakly above the water. My stomach drops. No fucking way. No no no.\r\n“Kevin!” my mother screams so loudly it startles birds from somewhere overhead. Of course. She scrambles toward the edge of the raft so suddenly it nearly tips us sideways.\r\n“Kevin! Baby!” The figure drifts closer with the waves. I slowly start to make out sunburned skin, cracked lips, a half-conscious body. But unmistakably Kevin. He’s sprawled across a splintered piece of driftwood like the world’s most annoying castaway. My mother starts sobbing immediately.\r\n“Oh my God. Oh my God!” I just stare. Some deeply selfish part of me had already buried him. Not because I wanted him dead, but because things had gotten quieter without him. Kevin coughs violently as we pull him onto the raft. The second he collapses beside my mother, the atmosphere changes. Like someone flipped a switch. Regina cradles his face in both hands.\r\n“My baby,” she whispers. Kevin blinks weakly. Then notices me sitting across from him. His face twists instantly.\r\n“You,” he rasps. Oh good. Near death hasn’t improved his personality.\r\n“Mhm,” I say.\r\n“What the hell happened?”\r\n“The ship sank, Kevin.”\r\n“No shit.” There he is. My mother quickly unscrews one of the water rations.\r\n“Here, baby.” I watch her hand him the bottle. Interesting. Considering I’m the one who collected the rainwater. Kevin drinks greedily before finally looking around the raft. He takes in the fishing line, knife, mackerel bones and supplies stacked neatly. His eyes narrow slightly.\r\n“You did all this?”\r\nBefore I can answer, Regina cuts in.\r\n“She’s actually been very useful.” Actually. Very useful. Wow. Somebody alert the press. Kevin looks at me like he’s trying to process the sentence physically. Then he laughs. Or tries to. It comes out more like a dying seal coughing into a pillow.\r\n“Who would’ve thought you could actually be helpful?” Something cold settles into my stomach. There it is. The old feeling. Like no matter what I do, they’ll always find a way to make me smaller again. Kevin leans back against the raft weakly.\r\n“You’ve been talking back to Mom?” Regina immediately looks uncomfortable. \r\n“She threw food overboard,” She says flatly. Traitor.\r\n“She was being insane,” I snap before I can stop myself. Kevin stares at me. Then slowly smiles. Not a nice smile. A Kevin smile.\r\n“You’re still a freak, huh?” And just like that, I understand. Nothing changed. Not the shipwreck. Not the ocean. Not survival. Not me saving her life. Nothing. If civilization disappeared tomorrow, my family would still somehow find a way to make me the problem. The raft falls quiet except for the sound of water slapping against rubber. Then I lower my eyes.\r\n“You’re right,” I say softly. Kevin blinks.\r\n“What?”\r\n“I’ve been stressed,” I murmur. “I shouldn’t have talked to Mom like that.” My mother visibly relaxes. Kevin smirks.\r\n“There she is.” I smile weakly back. Inside the supply crate beside me, wrapped carefully in torn emergency blanket fabric, rests the tiny translucent sac I removed from the pufferfish yesterday. For the first time since catching it, I stop thinking about it like a scientific curiosity. And start thinking about it like an option.\r\n________________\r\nPublic Execution\r\nThe red pen flows mindlessly between my fingers. It’s independent work time in Chemistry and I finished my assignment already, given I have no distractions like friends. My index finger and thumb expertly manipulate the Bic Intensity Rollerball, animating a lifeless thing between my digits. It’s almost mesmerizing, how my own fingers control every aspect of this pen. Where it has been, where it’s going, the angle it’s at at any moment. \r\nMy thoughts are interrupted by a giggle that comes from behind me. I hear it from my right, maybe a few rows behind me. Wait, that’s where Bradley sits. I cautiously turn my head, and see him looking right at me. I whip my head around and blush. There’s no way Bradley Carter is finally noticing me. I quickly check my sleeping phone’s reflection and bare my teeth at it. Good, nothing stuck in between them. I take myself in. I see my blunt bangs that don’t suit my frizzy hair. I look down and see one of the frumpy sweaters Mom exclusively lets me wear. Apparently, Steve Jobs had a daily uniform, and so should I. \r\nI turn back around. He’s still looking at me, whispering to the girl at the next desk this time. She’s also looking at me, smiling. Oh my god. Is he actually telling people he likes me now? I can’t help but beam and blush as I plan my next move. I take one last look at my bangs and teeth and tip my chin down just slightly to sniff myself. I didn’t shower this morning, but I did last night. Looks like that shower’s 24 hour freshness guarantee didn’t fail me. Perfect. I smooth out my bangs one last time and take a deep breath. I get out of my seat and turn around, putting on my most charming smile. I have a good feeling about this. I stride to Bradley's desk, and he doesn’t take his eyes off me. My heart flutters. Could this really be it? I approach his desk. \r\n“Hi, Bradley.” I say with as much confidence as I can muster. \r\n“What’s up, Mandy?” He looks pleased, a smirk playing on his lips. \r\n“Oh nothing, ha. But thank you for asking.” I smile, and his smirk grows wider.\r\n“Um. I guess I was wondering,” I continue.\r\n“Yes?”\r\n“Well, I guess I saw you smiling at me, and well I was wondering if you wanted to hang.. Um. Hang..” My words falter as I slowly notice the girls around him. They’re all looking at me too. Smiling. Not in a ‘go, girly!’ kinda way though. More like angler fish who just lured their next victim to their demise. \r\n“Hang out?” Bradley interrupts my thoughts, his smirk slowly growing into a jeer. The girls snicker. \r\n“Yeah?” I say, unsure of what trap I just stepped into.\r\n“Mandy, you actually thought I would want to hang out with you?” His words cut into my skin like a blade.\r\n“We were looking at you because you’ve had a piece of gum in your hair since second period.” Looks like public executions are still legal. All the blood drains out of my face. \r\n“What?” I muster. Bradley doubles down in laughter, giving his cohort permission to cackle. He looks up at me with genuine tears in his eyes. I look back at him, with tears in mine as well. I don’t even know what to say. I’m paralyzed in horror. \r\nAll of a sudden, I hear, “Amanda Li! What are you doing out of your seat?” Fuck me. I turn around and see Ms. Johnston glaring at me.\r\n“Sorry, Miss.” I look down. I feel their stares drilling into the back of my head. Honestly something drilling into my head would be less painful than this. \r\n“Mhm. Back to your seat now. I expected better from you Mandy. I thought I could count on you not to socialize.” I stare at her in disbelief. I hear a howl from Bradley’s desk. Glad someone found that funny. I go back to my seat and sit as still as possible for the rest of the period. I can’t explain why, but I throw away that pen at the end of class. \r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\nFamily Dinner\r\nDinner that night is weirdly pleasant. Not genuinely pleasant, obviously. More like the kind of pleasantness people perform in hospital waiting rooms and hostage videos. Kevin sits propped against the edge of the raft wrapped in an emergency blanket while Mom fusses over him constantly.\r\n“You need more water.”\r\n“I’m fine, Mom.”\r\n“No, you’re pale.”\r\n“We’re all pale.” I sit across from them gutting fish in silence. Every now and then I catch my mother glancing at me. Not lovingly. Carefully. Like she’s trying to figure out whether I’m still her daughter or some kind of wild animal that wandered onto the raft wearing my face. Fair question. I finish slicing the fish and hand Kevin the larger portion. Mom notices immediately. \r\n“See?” she says softly to him. “Your sister’s calming down.” Calming down. Like I’m a dog that stopped barking. Kevin snorts. \r\n“Guess attempted starvation wasn’t really her thing.” I force a laugh.\r\n“Yeah. Guess not.” Inside my stomach, guilt twists violently. Because despite everything, despite the ocean and the ship and the diary and Bradley and Kevin and my mother and every horrible thing that ever happened in my stupid little life— I still know this is wrong. That’s the annoying part. If I were a real psychopath this would probably feel way less complicated. Kevin takes another bite.\r\n“So,” he says through a mouthful of fish, “when we get rescued, are you still going to psycho camp?” Mom sighs.\r\n“Kevin…”\r\n“What? Dad already paid tuition.”\r\nI stare down at my hands. Blood from the fish stains the creases of my fingers red.\r\n“You know,” Kevin continues, “this whole thing might actually help your application essays.” Mom laughs quietly despite herself. Something hot flashes through my chest.\r\n“You could write about resilience,” he says mockingly. “‘Despite surviving a maritime disaster, I remained ugly and mentally unstable.’” Mom winces. Not because he’s being cruel. Because he said ugly out loud.\r\n“Kevin,” she murmurs.\r\n“What? She knows.” I feel suddenly very far away from my own body. The ocean rocks gently beneath us. Water slaps softly against rubber. Kevin keeps eating. Mom keeps watching me. And slowly, horrifyingly, I realize neither of them actually thinks I’m capable of hurting them. Even now. Even after all this. I look down at the remaining fish beside me. The tiny translucent sac hidden beneath the cutting cloth catches the moonlight faintly. My hands start shaking. Don’t do this. Then Kevin smirks again.\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nDear Diary\r\nBy the time I get home, I have already cut most of the gum out of my hair with safety scissors from the nurse’s office. Most. A small pink clump still clings stubbornly near the back of my head, which feels symbolic in a way I do not currently have the energy to unpack. I open the front door quietly, hoping to make it upstairs before anyone notices me. Naturally, the entire family is waiting in the dining room. Mom sits at the table with her face buried in her hands. Dad stands behind her, one hand on her shoulder, looking like a hostage negotiator who forgot his lines. Kevin sits beside her with his arms crossed, with that familiar smirk I despise playing at the corners of his mouth.\r\nIn front of them, open on the table, is my diary. My stomach drops to my butt.\r\n“Mom,” I say carefully. “Why do you have that?” She lifts her head. Her eyes are red. Her face is blotchy. Her mouth trembles like she’s the one who got publicly executed in chemistry today.\r\n“How could you do this to me?” she whispers. I stare at her. Wow. That was fast. Not:\r\nAre you okay? Not: Mandy, we need to talk. Not even: Why did you write this? Just straight back to her. Efficient, if nothing else.\r\n“You read my diary?”\r\n“That is what you’re worried about?” Her voice rises. “That is your concern right now?”\r\n“It’s private.”\r\n“Oh, private.” Kevin snorts. “Yeah, because privacy is definitely the main issue here.” Dad sighs.\r\n“Mandy, your mother is very upset.” Of course she is. Everyone is always very upset except me, who is apparently just decorative. Mom grabs the diary and flips through the pages with shaking hands.\r\n“Do you have any idea how humiliating it is to have your own son come to you with something like this?” Mom demands. I look at Kevin, and his smirk only widens. Of course. Kevin.\r\n“Do you know what it feels like to read your own daughter writing that she wishes she could disappear?” I say nothing. Because yes. I do know what that feels like. I wrote it. Mom presses a hand to her chest.\r\n“After everything we’ve done for you. The tutors. The private lessons. The enrichment programs. The clothes. The school. The opportunities.”\r\n“The sweaters,” I add before I can stop myself. Kevin laughs once. Mom glares at me.\r\n“You think this is funny?”\r\n“No.” \r\n“You think making threats like this is funny?”\r\n“They weren’t threats.”\r\n“Then what were they?” I look at the diary on the table. The pages are bent now. Some of them have little sticky tabs on them. Fucking sticky tabs. She annotated my nervous breakdown.\r\n“I was just writing,” I say. Kevin leans forward.\r\n“You wrote, ‘Sometimes I think everyone would be happier if I sank to the bottom of the ocean and never came back up.’” He says it in a dramatic little voice. Like he’s auditioning for the role of my final straw. Mom starts crying harder. Dad looks away.\r\n“Mandy,” he says quietly, “you shouldn’t write things like that down.” I look at him for a second, waiting for him to become my father. He does not. Mom wipes her face with the back of her hand.\r\n“Do you understand what people would think if they saw this? Do you understand how this makes us look?” There it is. The real family emergency. Not that I wanted to kill myself. That someone might notice.\r\n“I didn’t show anyone,” I say.\r\n“But you could have,” she snaps. “A teacher could have found this. A counselor. Another parent. Do you want people to think we abuse you?” The word sits on the table between us. Abuse. Nobody touches it. Kevin breaks the silence first.\r\n“Oh my God,” he says. “Are you trying to get Mom and Dad in trouble?”\r\n“No.”\r\n“Then why write all this psycho stuff?”\r\n“Kevin,” Dad says weakly. Not enough to stop him. Just enough to document that he tried. Kevin turns another page.\r\n“Here’s another one. ‘Mushu is the only one in this house who looks happy to see me.’”\r\n“That’s enough,” I say. My voice comes out smaller than I want it to. Kevin smiles.\r\n“Aw. Is the cat your best friend?” Something in my face must change, because Mom suddenly slams the diary shut.\r\n“Enough,” she says. For one stupid second, I think she means enough hurting me. Then she looks at me.\r\n“Clearly, we have been too lenient.” I laugh. It comes out wrong. Too sharp. Too close to crying.\r\n“Lenient?”\r\n“You need structure,” she says. “Real structure. Not this constant self-pity.”\r\n“Mom, please. I just had a really bad day.”\r\n“We all have bad days, Mandy. We don’t all write suicide notes and leave them around the house.”\r\n“It was in my drawer.” Mom ignores that. Dad clears his throat.\r\n“Your mother and I have been discussing options.” Options. That’s never a good word. “We found a boarding program,” Mom says.\r\nMy mouth goes dry. “No.”\r\n“It’s a very respected school.”\r\n“No.”\r\n“Strict academics. Mandarin immersion. Very strong discipline.”\r\n“Mom, please.” I’m one step away from getting on my knees. Kevin whistles.\r\n“Damn. Chinese boarding school. That’s crazy.” Mom shoots him a look, but not a real one. Just a warning to stop enjoying it visibly.\r\n“We’re not doing this to punish you,” Dad says.\r\n“Yes, you are.”\r\n“We’re doing this because we love you,” Mom says. That one almost makes me laugh again. Love, apparently, has uniforms. Love has curfews. Love has tuition deposits and behavioral correction plans. Love reads your diary and calls it betrayal. Mom folds her hands on the table.\r\n“We leave for the cruise next semester. One last family vacation before the school year starts.”\r\n“A cruise?” I repeat incredulously. \r\n“It’ll be good for us,” Dad says. “A reset.” \r\n“Try not to write any poems about jumping overboard.” Kevin grins. Nobody laughs. Nobody corrects him either. Mom slides my diary across the table toward herself, not me.\r\n“I’m keeping this.”\r\n“What? Why?”\r\n“Because clearly you cannot be trusted with your own thoughts.” I stand there in the dining room with gum still in my hair and my diary under my mother’s hand and my whole family looking at me like I’m something that happened to them. They never saw me as a person, but a problem. I want to scream. I want to cry. I want to flip the table. Instead, I nod. Because if this family has taught me anything, it’s that survival starts with acting sorry.\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nThicker Than Water\r\nKevin’s body stays between us for almost six hours. Neither of us suggests moving him. The raft drifts through black water under a swollen moon while my brother’s corpse slowly stiffens beside the supply crate, one arm dangling awkwardly over the edge like he might wake up and scratch his nose any second now. Mom sits curled against the opposite side of the raft with her knees pulled to her chest. I sit near the bow clutching the spear across my lap. Neither of us sleeps. The ocean sloshes softly beneath us. Somewhere far away, thunder rolls. Every now and then Mom looks at Kevin. Then at me. Then quickly away again. Like eye contact might provoke me. I can still hear Kevin’s final words in my head. You psychopath. It’s honestly impressive dedication to hating me. Most people stop insulting someone while actively dying. Blood dries sticky across my hands. I keep staring at it. Fish blood. Kevin blood.\r\nMine. Hard to tell where one starts and the other ends anymore. Mom suddenly clears her throat.\r\n“Mandy.” My entire body tenses instantly. Not outwardly. Internally. Like every organ inside me clenches at once.\r\n“What?” I ask quietly. Her eyes flick briefly toward Kevin again. Then back to me.\r\n“You need to clean your wounds.” I blink.\r\n“Huh?” \r\n“There’s alcohol wipes in the first aid kit.” I say nothing. The politeness between us feels almost funny. Like we’re coworkers trapped in an elevator instead of a mother and daughter sitting beside a dead child floating in the Pacific Ocean. Finally I laugh weakly. Mom flinches at the sound immediately. Interesting.\r\n“You know,” I murmur, “this is probably the longest conversation we’ve ever had that wasn’t about my grades.”\r\n“Mandy…”\r\n“No seriously.” My voice shakes. “This might actually be a bonding moment for  us.” Mom’s eyes start watering instantly.\r\n“I know you hate me right now.” I stare at her. Hate. Such a small word for something this large. The raft rocks harder suddenly as a wave rolls beneath us. Kevin’s body shifts slightly. Mom makes a horrible choking sound in her throat. Then she starts crying again. Not loud dramatic crying like before. Quiet crying. Exhausted crying. The kind that sounds like someone drowning slowly.\r\n“I tried so hard,” she whispers. Something twists painfully in my chest. Because against all logic, against every sane instinct in my body, part of me still wants to comfort her. That’s the sickest part of all this.\r\n“I know,” I say quietly. Mom looks up sharply like she didn’t expect kindness from me anymore. \r\n“I just…” Her breathing shakes violently. “I wanted both of you to succeed.” Both of you. Interesting that Kevin still made the cut.\r\n“I know,” I repeat.\r\n“And when you were little…” She wipes at her face. “You used to be so sweet.”\r\nI laugh once under my breath.\r\n“There it is.”\r\n“What?”\r\n“You only ever talk about me in past tense.”\r\nMom’s face crumples.\r\n“Mandy, please.”\r\n“No, seriously.” My voice gets sharper. “You talk about me like I died years ago.”\r\n“That’s not true.”\r\n“Then when was the last time you actually liked me?” Silence. Just ocean. Just waves. Just Kevin’s corpse between us. Mom opens her mouth. Closes it. And somehow that hurts more than anything else tonight.\r\n“I loved you,” she says finally. Loved. Past tense again. I start crying immediately. I hate myself for it. Huge ugly sobs tear out of my chest while I try desperately to stay quiet. Mom slowly inches closer toward me across the raft. Very carefully. Like approaching a wounded dog.\r\n“Mandy,” she whispers. I can’t look at her. Because part of me still wants this. Still wants her to hold me. Still wants her to finally choose me.\r\n“I’m sorry,” she says. My breath catches. For one horrible second— I believe her. I actually fucking believe her. Mom reaches out slowly and touches my cheek. Gentle. The gentlest she’s touched me in years. My whole body starts to tremble.\r\n“I love you,” she whispers. And that’s when I notice her other hand moving. Fast. Metal flashes in the moonlight. Pain explodes through my face. I scream so hard my throat tears raw. The fishhook rips through my lower lip and catches deep into my cheek before tearing free. She lunges again wildly. I throw my arm up instinctively. The second hook buries into my forearm. Barbed metal sinks deep.\r\n“Oh my God!” Mom yanks backward with all her strength. The hook tears through flesh. I feel skin split open. Hot blood pours instantly down my arm.\r\n“Mandy!” Mom screams hysterically. “Stay away from me!” Stay away from her? Something inside me ruptures. Not snaps. Ruptures. I slam into her blindly. The raft tilts violently beneath us. Mom shrieks as we crash against the supply crate. The spear clatters loose beside Kevin’s body. Mom sees it first. So do I. For one horrible second we both freeze. Then we lunge simultaneously. Mom grabs the shaft first. I grab the other end. We wrestle violently across the raft while blood slicks my hands so badly I can barely hold on.\r\n“Mandy stop!” she screams.\r\n“You tried to kill me!”\r\n“You poisoned my son!” Your son. Always your son. Never my brother. Rage detonates through me so suddenly I almost black out. Mom jerks the spear sideways violently and the butt slams directly into my split lip. White-hot agony blinds me. I scream and shove forward with everything I have. Mom loses balance instantly. Her eyes widen in genuine terror as she tumbles backward over the edge of the raft. Then— Splash. Silence. For one impossible second, the ocean swallows her completely. Then she surfaces choking. “Mandy!” she gasps frantically. The spear remains clenched in my hands. Mom grabs weakly at the side of the raft. I could pull her back up. I know I could. And horrifyingly,  part of me still wants to. “Mandy please,” she sobs. “Please.” Blood drips from my chin into the ocean below. My whole body shakes violently. I stare down at her. At my mother. My entire childhood floating there in the water looking suddenly old and small and terrified. Then her expression changes. Just slightly. Not fear anymore. Blame. Like this is still somehow my fault. And suddenly I’m nine years old again holding crushed tiger lilies while Kevin laughs beside her. I’m fourteen standing beside the dining table while she reads my suicide diary out loud. I’m fifteen watching her hand Kevin the bigger piece of fish. Something inside me finally goes still. Slowly, I kneel beside the supply crate. Mom sees the tiny translucent poison sac in my hand. And starts screaming. “No no no no— Mandy please!” I’m crying so hard I can barely breathe.\r\n“What can I say, Mom?” My voice breaks violently. “I learned from the best.” Then I squeeze the pufferfish venom into her mouth. Mom thrashes instantly. Water splashes wildly around her. Her fingers claw desperately at the raft. At me. At anything. Tetrodotoxin works slowly. That’s the cruel part. Her movements gradually weaken one by one. First her hands. Then her legs. Then her jaw. All while she stays completely conscious. Her eyes lock onto mine the entire time. Terrified. Accusing. Begging. Then eventually— stillness. The ocean pulls her gently backward into darkness. I sit alone on the raft shaking so hard my teeth chatter while Kevin’s body lies beside me and my mother disappears beneath the surface of the water she spent my entire life warning me about. And somewhere deep inside me, the little girl who wanted her mother’s love finally drowns too. I guess what they say is true: Monsters aren’t born. They’re created. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nEpilogue\r\nBy the time I’m seventeen, the internet starts calling me The Pacific Princess. Creative. News stations eat the story up immediately.\r\n\r\n\r\nSOLE SURVIVOR OF CRUISE DISASTER.\r\n        TEENAGE GIRL SURVIVES 19 DAYS ADRIFT.\r\n        THE REAL-LIFE LIFE OF PI.\r\n\r\n\r\nThat last one pisses me off especially because I had the book first. People become obsessed with me in a way that feels vaguely religious. They invite me onto talk shows. Podcasts. Documentaries. Everyone wants to hear about survival. Nobody wants to hear about what came before it. Apparently audiences prefer inspirational trauma. I learn quickly that if you stare just past the interviewer and laugh softly before saying something vague about resilience, people start crying. Humans are honestly incredibly easy to manipulate. The scar splitting my lower lip heals into something sharp and pale. Makeup artists try to cover it at first, but audiences actually like it better visible. \r\n“It makes you look strong,” one stylist tells me. Interesting. My mother spent sixteen years trying to make me prettier. Turns out all I needed was attempted murder. I become weirdly fashionable after that. People online start copying my blunt bangs. My sweaters. My scar. A girl on TikTok literally calls me “ethereal-looking.” If sixteen-year-old me heard that sentence, she would probably burst into tears on the spot. Sometimes I still expect to wake up back on the raft. Salt in my mouth. Kevin beside me. Mom staring at me like she finally understood what I was capable of. \r\nInstead I wake up in hotel rooms. Penthouse apartments. Photoshoots. The world loves a beautiful survivor. Especially one who knows how to smile at the right moments. The funniest part is Bradley. Apparently public humiliation builds character because he starts messaging me roughly three months after my first interview goes viral. At first it’s harmless. Hey Mandy, glad you’re okay. Then: You look amazing lately. Then: We should catch up sometime. I leave him on read for six consecutive weeks. Today he shows up in person. I’m leaving a bookstore signing when I hear someone say my name behind me.\r\n“Mandy?” I turn. And there he is. Bradley Carter. Slightly taller. Slightly broader. Same stupid face. Funny how some things survive disasters untouched. He smiles nervously when I look at him. In his hands is a bouquet of tiger lilies. For a second I genuinely can’t breathe. Not because it’s romantic. Because it feels like seeing a ghost hold your childhood.\r\n“They’re your favorite, right?” he says. The flowers glow orange beneath the city lights. Tiger lilies. Beautiful. Poisonous to cats. I stare at them quietly. Bradley laughs awkwardly.\r\n“I know this is probably weird,” he says. “I just… I always kinda regretted what happened back then.” Back then. Interesting phrase. Like humiliation has a statute of limitations. Like people stop carrying it once enough years pass.\r\n“I was an asshole,” he admits. Yep. You glued gum into my hair and laughed while I cried in front of thirty people, but sure. Past tense.\r\n“You probably hate me.” Mhm. I look at him for a long moment. The old version of me would’ve folded instantly. She would’ve taken the flowers home and pressed the petals between textbook pages like sacred artifacts. She would’ve mistaken being perceived for being loved. But that girl drowned a long time ago. Instead I smile politely. The exact kind of smile interviewers love.\r\n“I don’t hate you, Bradley.” His shoulders visibly relax. I take the tiger lilies gently from his hands. They’re beautiful up close. Bright orange with black-speckled petals curling outward like flames. For a second, I think about keeping them. Then I remember another backyard. Another pair of hands crushing them into dirt.\r\n“They’re my favorite flower,” I say softly. Bradley smiles.\r\n“I remembered.”\r\n“I know.” I turn slightly and drop the bouquet into the trash can beside us. His smile falters immediately.\r\n“Mandy?”\r\n“My mother used to hate these,” I continue calmly. “She said they were poisonous. Dangerous. Bad to keep around living things.” Bradley stares at me silently. Camera flashes erupt somewhere down the street. For a moment neither of us moves. Then I smooth the sleeve over the scar on my arm and smile the polished practiced smile the public adores so much.\r\n“But I think that’s what I like about them.”\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nThe Bell Was Missing\r\nMango disappears on a Thursday.\r\nMom tapes photocopied flyers to telephone poles around the neighborhood while Dad shakes a bag of treats on the porch every night like the cat might come trotting back if they just believe hard enough. Mandy cries harder than anyone. Hard enough that I almost feel guilty for suspecting her at all.\r\nAlmost. Because Mango hated going outside. Even when the front door hung open, the cat would just sit there twitching his tail against the tile like the outdoors offended him personally.\r\n“He probably got picked up by somebody,” Mom says eventually. “People take cats in all the time.” I nod, but I stop sleeping with my bedroom door open after that.\r\nA few weeks later, I’m digging through Mandy’s room looking for the TV remote she stole from me. The room smells faintly like nail polish and something sour underneath it. Clothes spill out of half-open drawers. There are candy wrappers shoved between the bedframe slats.\r\n“Kevin?” Mandy calls from downstairs. I freeze.\r\n“Yeah?” I answer.\r\n“Did you take my charger?”\r\n“No.” I keep looking.\r\nThe remote isn’t under the bed, but a little tin box is. One of those cheap metal ones with faded flowers painted on top. I pull it out absentmindedly, pop it open—and stare. Inside is Mango’s collar. The tiny brass bell is gone. For a second, I just kneel there on the carpet holding it. I trace the faded orange fabric. The frayed stitching near the buckle where Mango used to scratch at it with his back foot.\r\n“Kevin!” Mandy yells again, closer this time. I shove the collar back into the tin so fast my fingers fumble. My hands start sweating. Suddenly my chest tightens. I slide the box back under the bed exactly where I found it. That night, Mandy sits beside me on the couch eating popsicles and laughing at cartoons like nothing happened. Red syrup stains the corners of her mouth. I watch her hands the whole time. After that, whenever Mandy picks up Mushu, I tell her to put the cat down. She rolls her eyes every time.\r\n“Relax,” she says. “I’m not gonna kill him.”\r\nNobody understands why I start sleeping with Mushu in my room after that."}];
